```cpp
#include <sstream>
#include <string>
#include <chrono>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Convert Markdown to HTML
    std::ostringstream htmlString;
    htmlString << "<?";
    htmlString << markdown;
    htmlString << "```";
    return htmlString.str();
}
```

This function uses the `std::ostringstream` class to create a string representation of the Markdown text, encapsulated in an HTML tag. The HTML text is then returned as a `std::string`.

Note that this function assumes that the Markdown text is properly formatted and does not contain any special characters. If the Markdown text is not properly formatted, the resulting HTML text will be incorrect.

Here is an example use case of the function:

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string markdown = "```Hello World!";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This code will output:

```
?>Hello World!```
```

Note that the output is not a valid HTML string, but rather a representation of the Markdown text encapsulated in an HTML tag.

